-- allium: 3
-- Ordered secondary indexes over resolved table events.
-- Table events and capture follow db.allium and allium/live-index.allium.
-- See rust/crates/node/src/ordered_index.

entity Definition {
    identity: String
    generation: Integer
    columns: List<String>
}

entity Checkpoint {
    definition: Definition
    frontier: Integer
    page_scope: Integer
    source_rows: Integer
}

-- Invariants
--
-- Repeating a declaration with IF NOT EXISTS preserves the definition and its generation.
-- A checkpoint covers the table catalog's durable event count at its frontier.
-- The checkpoint becomes usable after every referenced page is durable.
-- A manifest read admits the selected extent before fetching its body and matches the physical version when the storage owner exposes one.
-- Immutable publication evidence confirms a checkpoint's exact bytes before reclamation begins.
-- A checkpoint root owns its retained pointer backing after manifest decode workspace is released.
-- A root contains each winning indexed row over disjoint valid-time spans at its system-time point.
-- Aligned bucket decomposition places an eligible row in exactly one of the prefixes containing a valid-time point.
-- A revision edits both sides of a changed projection. Unchanged spans retain their postings.
-- Plain scalar keys project from selected positions in the source Arrow columns.
-- Retained projected tuples own compact key batches and their memory reservations.
-- A contiguous, all-valid selection may share compact, layout-compatible Arrow buffers whose backing capacity is known.
-- A governed put binds its typed Arrow identity to the borrowed identity in its event header.
-- A Binary identity in an ordered tuple compares by its complete unsigned bytes and agrees with its typed posting handle.
-- A world shadow's logical null field marker identifies a local tombstone.
-- Leaf edits and correction writes select key slots from their Arrow columns across plain and dense-union layouts.
-- Each rebuilt slot derives its layout from selected rows and assigns dense-union offsets in selection order.
-- Compact contiguous selections may share the backing allocations of index-owned Arrow keys.
-- A rebuilt leaf transfers its construction grant to new backing and retains the existing owners of shared buffers.
-- A correction page serializes just the Arrow key rows for the validity spans it owns.
-- Ordered leaf publication writes canonical IPC body buffers into the final page allocation.
-- A decoded correction group shares the admitted page backing with its validity spans.
-- The read opens a root at its system-time point and applies captured uncovered events, including erasures.
-- A snapshot may reuse decoded index definitions for the same catalog read time.
-- A snapshot may reuse projected tail changes from the same definition and captured source block.
-- A read or maintenance cycle owns index page buffers for its own lifetime.
-- A reachability walk releases each opened page after visiting its references unless the page already belongs to the cycle's seek cache.
-- A point read merges bucket prefixes in key order.
-- Readers of the shared layout open JSON and Arrow IPC branch pages, revision directories, and checkpoint manifests.
-- Shared-layout Arrow reference publication starts after each reader in the installation can open both formats.
-- A process-private in-memory store can publish Arrow references by default.
-- Other durable or shared stores require the writer's explicit setting to publish Arrow references.
-- An explicit JSON selection takes precedence over the shared-layout publication default.
-- A legacy checkpoint checksum binds the canonical JSON layout published by the writer.
-- A shared-layout Arrow page can keep a reference to a page originally written in JSON.
-- A runtime-installed journal's serving and exact-snapshot attachments select a distinct ordered-index layout address.
-- The runtime-installed layout uses Arrow for every published manifest and reachable page.
-- Maintenance builds the runtime-installed layout from captured sources when its checkpoint is absent.
-- A reader validates immutable page size and digest before interpreting the page's format.
-- Arrow references bind the measured subtree count, domain, and coefficient alongside the full key fences.
-- One Arrow reference row binds the pointer identity once and carries its first and last typed fences separately.
-- An Arrow posting fence can share its address bytes under the reservation already held by its key tuple.
-- Every immutable page reference binds its exact subtree entry count and full fences.
-- A covered held count subtracts the full-key boundary ranks in each bucket containing the requested valid instant.
-- The bucket cover makes the counts disjoint across the visible postings for that instant.
-- Unsigned Arrow cells compare at their signed execution widths for bounds, seeks, and page order.
-- Zoned microsecond timestamp keys compare by their instant for bounds, seeks, and page order.
-- Floating-point ordering distinguishes signed zeros and ties every NaN last. Zero and NaN bounds are left to the row predicate, and NaNs match no indexed comparison.
-- A covered point read streams typed batches of the requested columns from those postings after applying every predicate.
-- A negotiated standalone held range reply emits complete Arrow IPC messages under the original witness and lifetime release checks.
-- A payload seek applies remaining predicates to batches resolved at the captured basis, then gathers the qualifying batch and row offsets in index order.
-- A seek evaluates each bound expression once for its comparison keys.
-- A held range compares variable-width selector values within their caller's lifetime.
-- A point scan funds merge heads by bucket count, tree frames as paths grow, and result staging by batch size.
-- A candidate scope has one sorted Arrow IID column for membership and range intersection.
-- Filtering and output use the same resolved payload batches.
-- Gathering nested payloads preserves their source Arrow vectors and composes their selected positions in index order.
-- A contiguous forward run of plain text, binary, UUID, Boolean, or microsecond instant cells may share its selected value bytes.
-- A contiguous forward covered result may share its byte-aligned validity bits.
-- The result's bitmaps expose only bits belonging to selected rows.
-- Hosted shared buffers retain the page permit through synchronous wire encoding and drop before transport waits.
-- Hosted APIs returning Arrow arrays to callers use independent result buffers.
-- An ordered distinct page filters eligible rows before counting each distinct sort prefix once.
-- All participating equality classes must be adjacent in the index order.
-- On the ordinary fallback plan, an input-opening error takes precedence over an OFFSET or FETCH error.
-- After the input opens, OFFSET is checked before FETCH.
-- An erasure removes the IID's old membership from every affected root and makes unreachable pages collectible.
-- Replay discovers captured erase cuts before it writes pages, and projects each IID from its latest erase onward.
-- Replay can retain decoded input pages through erase discovery and key projection within its memory budget.
-- Replay validates every input row's event controls before projecting selected rows in bounded chunks.
-- Revisions assembled in the same replay share each complete identity's retained bytes and reservation.
-- When retention is declined, replay can read exact IPC blocks from the held source in file order.
-- Each range agrees with the observed file identity, and the replay releases its backing after key projection.
-- A checkpoint's page scope is zero before the first captured erase, and one past the last source block containing an erase afterward.
-- Every page reachable from a checkpoint uses that checkpoint's scope. Page addresses within the scope retain their source block.
-- After the replacement checkpoint is published, collection releases older scopes and preserves pages staged in newer scopes.
-- Data compaction preserves input events and changes no ordered-index checkpoint.
-- A millisecond date key uses the UTC day containing its value, including for negative milliseconds.
-- Date32 and Date64 values on the same day use the same epoch-day key.
-- The layout version participates in every definition's derived address.
-- Older postings with truncating Date64 keys are bypassed while the new layout is built.
-- Replacing a layout retires the former address with the same storage fences as a dropped definition.
